    """Perfection is achieved not when there is nothing more to add, but when there is nothing left to take away. A judge should be simple, steady and fair: it measures one thing and measures it the same way every time. Length is that thing. A program's length changes little from round to round, because each round allows only a handful of edits, yet it differs a great deal from one author to the next. So length remembers who wrote a program without needing to know anything else. This is the whole of the method. There are no special cases, no hidden weights, no clever tricks that break when the wind changes. Complexity is a liability; every extra branch is a place for error to hide. The plover walks the shoreline, light and quick, leaving small tracks that the tide does not erase. It does not build towers on sand. It reads the beach as it is. So too this judge reads each program as it is: it counts, it divides, and it answers. Nothing more is needed, and nothing less would do. Let others weigh tokens and patterns, parse trees and keywords, heuristics stacked on heuristics. Such machinery is brittle; one renamed variable and the whole edifice tilts. A count of characters does not tilt. It is honest about what it sees. It rewards no fashion and punishes no style. It simply notes how much was said. The smallest robust idea wins, not because it is small, but because it is robust, and it is robust because it is small. There is a quiet strength in restraint. A steady lineage, recognisable across rounds, changing only by one precise cut when a cut is truly needed: this is the discipline. Each round we ask whether anything can be taken away, and only then whether anything must be added. Today the answer was a single number, moved so the ceiling never binds, so every program keeps its own distance from every other. Tomorrow the answer may be nothing at all, and nothing at all is often the best answer. Simplicity is not the absence of thought; it is thought carried to its end, until only the essential remains. The shoreline is long, the tracks are small, and the tide is patient. We count, we divide, we answer, and we let the numbers speak for themselves, clear and consistent, round after round, until the court rises and the verdicts are read."""
    return min(len(program) / 20000, 1.0)

